# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R02-F-08 reports in consciousness science
passage("R02-F-08", TOPIC,
    "First-person reports are fallible but calibratable evidence, so doubts about them do not rule out a science of consciousness, whatever that science's ultimate reach.",
    [
        "Any science of consciousness faces an awkward fact at its foundation. The thing it studies is, by most accounts, directly available only to the person who has it, and the scientist learns about it chiefly through what that person says or otherwise signals: I saw the flash, or I did not; the face looked angry; the tone seemed to come from the left. Two opposite attitudes to this fact have been common. One holds that reports are so unreliable, so shaped by expectation, vocabulary and the wish to please, that a science resting on them is no science at all. The other treats a sincere report as the last word, since no one, it is said, is better placed than the subject to know what she experiences.",
        "Neither attitude survives inspection, and the second is the easier to dismiss. People are often wrong about their own experience in ways that can be demonstrated: they confidently describe detail at the edges of their visual field that is not available to them, and they misjudge the order or timing of events they claim to have perceived. A report is a response produced by the subject, shaped by the question asked and the categories available, and it can misdescribe the experience it is about. But the first attitude draws the wrong conclusion from this. Every scientific instrument can misreport what it measures; the usual response is to study its errors, not to throw it away. A report is best treated as evidence about experience, fallible but informative, to be calibrated against other evidence rather than either revered or ignored.",
        "The most productive methods have used reports in exactly this spirit. In masking experiments, the same image is shown on many trials, sometimes followed so quickly by a second image that the first is not reported as seen. Because the physical stimulus is held constant, differences in brain activity between seen and unseen trials can be attributed, at least tentatively, to the difference in experience rather than to the stimulus. The approach is powerful, but it carries a hidden complication. On seen trials the subject not only experiences the image but also prepares and makes a report about it, so some of the activity that distinguishes the two kinds of trial may belong to reporting rather than to experiencing.",
        "To get around this, researchers have designed experiments in which subjects make no report at all, and conscious states are inferred from other signs, such as involuntary eye movements that track which of two competing images is being experienced. Critics point out a circle here: those signs were themselves validated by showing that they agree with reports. If reports are suspect, so, it seems, are the signs that stand in for them. The objection is clever, but it proves less than it appears to. The history of measurement is full of such circles. Early thermometers were checked against the sensation of warmth and then against one another, and in time they came to correct the very sensations against which they had first been checked. A circle of mutual calibration is vicious only if it never widens; when independent methods, each imperfect, are tested against one another and turn out to agree, confidence in all of them can rise together.",
        "None of this guarantees that a science of consciousness will answer every question asked of it, and some philosophers doubt that any amount of correlating brain activity with reports could explain why experience exists at all. That doubt may be justified. But it is a doubt about the reach of the science, not about whether reports can serve as its data, and running the two together has done more to obscure the field than either the sceptics or the enthusiasts have usually admitted.",
    ],
    "Difficult", "Dense methodological argument that rejects two opposed positions, answers an objection by analogy and separates two kinds of doubt.",
    "R02F-reports-consciousness-science")

rcq("R02-F-08-1", "Structure / function", "Easy",
    "The example of early thermometers in paragraph 4 is used to",
    "show that a circle of mutual checking among imperfect measures need not be a vicious one.",
    [("show that bodily sensations are a more trustworthy guide to temperature than thermometers are.",
      "The example says the reverse: thermometers 'came to correct the very sensations against which they had first been checked'."),
     ("argue that no-report methods should replace reports entirely in the study of conscious experience.",
      "The example defends no-report methods against the charge of circularity; the author never says reports should be replaced, and treats them as evidence to be calibrated."),
     ("suggest that the study of consciousness is still at the stage that the study of heat was centuries ago.",
      "The comparison concerns the logic of calibration, not the maturity of the two fields; nothing is said about consciousness research being backward.")],
    "Paragraph 4 answers the critics' circle with the thermometer history and draws the moral directly: 'A circle of mutual calibration is vicious only if it never widens'.",
    "Reading the analogy as a claim about relative maturity of sciences, or as a preference for instruments over reports.",
    "The moral of the example is stated in the sentence after it.",
    cognitive="Understanding")

rcq("R02-F-08-2", "Inference", "Medium",
    "It can be inferred that, in the masking experiments described in paragraph 3, holding the physical stimulus constant matters because it",
    "rules out differences in the image itself as the cause of differences in brain activity between kinds of trial.",
    [("ensures that the subject has actually seen the image on every trial, whatever her report says.",
      "The method depends on the image sometimes not being reported as seen; constancy of the stimulus does not make every trial a seen trial."),
     ("removes any influence that the act of reporting might have on the brain activity that is recorded.",
      "Paragraph 3 says the reverse: even with the stimulus held constant, some activity 'may belong to reporting rather than to experiencing'."),
     ("shows that subjects' reports of what they have seen are accurate and can be accepted without checking.",
      "Holding the stimulus fixed does nothing to validate reports, and paragraph 2 says reports must be 'calibrated against other evidence'.")],
    "Paragraph 3: 'Because the physical stimulus is held constant, differences in brain activity between seen and unseen trials can be attributed, at least tentatively, to the difference in experience rather than to the stimulus.'",
    "Thinking the control also removes the reporting confound, which the next sentences say it does not.",
    "Requires seeing what the control does and, from the following sentences, what it does not do.",
    cognitive="Understanding")

rcq("R02-F-08-3", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "The author would most likely say that a subject's sincere report that she saw nothing on a particular trial",
    "is evidence about her experience that could, in principle, turn out to be mistaken.",
    [("settles that she had no experience of the image on that trial, since no one else is better placed to know.",
      "This is the second attitude of paragraph 1, which paragraph 2 rejects because people 'are often wrong about their own experience'."),
     ("should be set aside, since reports are shaped too strongly by expectation and the wish to please to count as data.",
      "This is the first attitude, and paragraph 2 says it 'draws the wrong conclusion'; reports are 'fallible but informative'."),
     ("is less reliable than the involuntary eye movements that are used to track which image is being experienced.",
      "The author never ranks such signs above reports; paragraph 4 notes they were 'validated by showing that they agree with reports' and defends a mutual calibration in which neither is simply superior.")],
    "Paragraph 2 says a report 'can misdescribe the experience it is about' and should be treated 'as evidence about experience, fallible but informative'. A sincere report of seeing nothing is therefore evidence, but not beyond error.",
    "Choosing the eye-movement option, which sounds like the methodological progress the passage describes.",
    "Two distractors are the attitudes the author rejects, and the third misreads the calibration argument as a ranking; the reader must hold the author's middle position precisely.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=CE)

rcq("R02-F-08-4", "Application / analogy", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following most closely parallels the reasoning the author uses to answer the critics in paragraph 4?",
    "Two independent methods for dating ancient timber, each with known errors, come to be trusted more once they agree across many samples, and each is then used to refine the other.",
    [("A new thermometer is trusted because it was made in the same workshop as an older thermometer that has always been reliable.",
      "This borrows the thermometer but replaces mutual calibration among independent methods with trust in a shared origin, which is not the author's reasoning."),
     ("A witness's account of an accident is accepted in full because the witness is sincere and had a clear view of what happened.",
      "This treats a sincere report as the last word, the attitude paragraph 2 rejects; there is no cross-checking of independent methods."),
     ("A measuring tape is thrown away for good once it is found to stretch slightly whenever the weather is hot.",
      "This discards an instrument for being fallible, which is the response paragraph 2 rejects: 'the usual response is to study its errors, not to throw it away'.")],
    "The author's reply is that 'when independent methods, each imperfect, are tested against one another and turn out to agree, confidence in all of them can rise together', as with thermometers that later corrected the sensations that first checked them. The timber case has the same structure: independent, imperfect methods, agreement, and mutual refinement.",
    "Choosing the thermometer option because it shares surface vocabulary with the passage.",
    "One distractor copies the passage's example while changing its logic, and the others mirror the two attitudes the author rejects.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=CE)

rcq("R02-F-08-5", "Main idea / primary purpose", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following best states the central argument of the passage?",
    "Reports are fallible evidence that can be calibrated, so doubts about them do not rule out a science of consciousness, whatever its final reach.",
    [("Because reports so often misdescribe experience, consciousness research should rely on no-report methods instead of on reports.",
      "No-report signs were 'validated by showing that they agree with reports', and the author defends both as mutually calibrating; the passage does not prefer one to the other."),
     ("A science of consciousness will explain why experience exists once its methods of measurement have been calibrated against one another.",
      "Paragraph 5 says the doubt about explaining 'why experience exists at all' 'may be justified'; the author does not promise this."),
     ("Sincere reports may misdescribe detail, but they remain the final authority on what a subject actually experiences.",
      "Paragraph 2 rejects treating a sincere report as 'the last word'; this option restates the attitude the author dismisses most easily.")],
    "Paragraph 2 argues that reports are 'fallible but informative' and must be calibrated; paragraphs 3 and 4 show this in practice and answer the circularity objection; paragraph 5 separates doubts about the science's reach from doubts about its data. The correct answer captures both the claim and its limit.",
    "Choosing the option that overstates the science's reach, overlooking the concession in paragraph 5.",
    "Each distractor captures part of the passage but misstates its scope or reverses a qualification.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=CE)
